Z||000022||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||11:04 AM||||Hey Cadaver,<P>  I am an electical engineer, and I have been into RC planes and cars for 20 years, and have built radio controlled robots for Dean Kamens FIRST competition high school teams<P>and I have designed and built high speed closed loop feedback control systems for large motors that moved large objects<P>  and I have no idea what the H#ll you are talking about!<P>  for RC applications you have a radio receiver that puts out a pulse train, proportional to the postion of the joysticks on the tranmitter<P> and you have DC motors with feedback potentiometers, with circuits that compair the shaft position to the pulse train.<P>  the motor and the feedback, and the control circuits combined are the 'servo'.<P>  there is no servo driver.<P>  maybe if you tell us what it is you are trying to do then we can give you some meaningful responses.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||11:10 AM||||something my nephew and I discovered a long time ago that is a TOTAL BLAST to do with RC cars - <P>  get a gallon plastic milk bottle, a quiet neighborhood street, pick two adjacent driveways to be the goal lines<P> and play RC car soccer! you start with the milk bottle in the center of the street, with both cars on opposite sides, backed up against the milk bottle. If the bottle crosses the line of the driveway it scores 1 point. If it goes off the road you start over from where it went out of bounds.<P>   expect your cars to get banged up. And expect to have a blast.<P>   4 or more cars is even better. It can get really wild.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000024||Ken Wittlief||06-12-2001||11:11 AM||||the other good thing about RC car soccer is really different cars can compete on an equal basis. Its not a matter of pure speed or cornering ability - driver skill and handling the unexpected situations are the keys.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||rogergarrison||06-12-2001||11:44 AM||||Ken, we used to 'rat racing' with our planes. you ever do that or even hear of it. You build a simple, ez to repair plane and tie a streamer to the tail, then everybody tries to chew off the others streamer. Not ez on airplanes. We had a club in columbus that built scale model warships too. They used to sell a remote/servo operated CO2 gun turrent that fired bb's. All we did was wrap electronics in sealed baggies inside the thin balsa boat hull, chose up sides and go to war. That was a blast. (do it in shallow water, so you can retrieve it by bouy later)||||reg||1
